 { Jon Ludvig Hammer – Rune Djurhuus, Norwegian Championship 2013 Learning objectives: This game illustrates three important elements in White’s strategy: 1. Opening the c-file and invading the c7-square with major pieces. 2. Trading dark-squared bishops to emphasize the weakness of the e5-square. 3. Occupying the weak e5-square with a knight. } 1. d4 e6 2. c4 f5 3. g3 Nf6 4. Bg2 ( { PH: This is actually inaccurate by today's standards! } 4. Nf3 d5 5. Nc3 $5 Bd6 6. Bf4 { is very annoying for Black, White not having developed the bishop to g2 yet. For instance } Bxf4 7. gxf4 O-O 8. e3 Qe7 9. h4 c6 10. h5 { and Black either has to allow h6 or play ...h6 and allow Ne5-g6. } ) 
c6 5. Nf3 d5 6. O-O Bd6 7. Qc2 O-O 8. Nc3 ( { Far less common is } 8. Bf4 { trading dark-squared bishops immediately, since after } Bxf4 9. gxf4 Ne4 { there is excellent counterplay despite the weak e5-square, for example: } 10. Nbd2 Nd7 11. e3 Qe7 12. Rac1 Kh8 { (planning ...Rg8 and an eventual ...g7-g5) } 13. Nxe4 fxe4 14. Nd2 g5 { when Black was better in Hakki – Ulibin, Dubai 2002. } ) 
Ne4 ( { The capture } 8... dxc4 $6 { is often a bad idea, as Black’s solid centre becomes vulnerable after } 9. e4 $1 Bc7 10. Qe2 fxe4 ( { or } 10... b5 11. exf5 exf5 12. a4 $1 { ± } ) 
11. Nxe4 Nxe4 12. Qxe4 $14 { with more than enough compensation. } ) 
 ( { PH: Back when this book was written, the combination of Qc2 and Nc3 (and then Rb1) was an attempt to refute the Stonewall. Some lines are: } 8... Qe7 9. Bg5 { , and now } Nbd7 { is awkward because after } 10. cxd5 { Black has to take back with the c-pawn, as } exd5 { drops f5. } ) 
 ( 8... a5 { is a more positional plan played by Kramnik recently, with the idea } 9. a3 Bd7 10. Rb1 b5 { , trying to get White to close the position with } 11. c5 Bc7 { . } ) 
9. Rb1 { Preparing a queenside expansion with c4-c5 and b2-b4-b5. } a5 ( { A healthy option was } 9... Nd7 10. b4 b5 $1 { stopping the expansion. For example after } 11. cxb5 Nxc3 12. Qxc3 cxb5 { (preparing ...Nb6-c4) and if } 13. Qc6 { Black is just in time with } Qb6 $1 14. Qxa8 Bb8 $1 { ∞ followed by ...Bb7 with a complex position as in Baryshpolets – Ponfilenok, St Petersburg 2012. } ) 
 ( { PH: After } 9... Qe7 { White can play } 10. b4 $1 { anyway, since } Bxb4 { is answered by } 11. Nxe4 fxe4 12. Bg5 $1 { and the b4-bishop will be exchanged for the knight on f3, giving White the pair of bishops and Black some dark squared weaknesses. } ) 
10. a3 Nd7 ( { The expansion cannot be prevented with } 10... Qe7 { since } 11. b4 $5 { comes anyway, and White is better after: } axb4 12. axb4 Bxb4 13. Nxe4 dxe4 14. Bg5 Qd7 15. Rxb4 exf3 16. exf3 $14 ) 
11. b4 axb4 12. axb4 Nxc3 13. Qxc3 b5 { Preventing White’s plan of c4-c5 followed by b4-b5, though creating new problems in the position. } ( { If } 13... Nf6 14. c5 Bc7 15. Ne5 $14 { the potential b4-b5 is annoying } ( { but } 14... b5 14. b5 $6 { is premature because of } Ne4 16. Qc2 cxb5 17. Rxb5 b6 { when Black fully equalizes. } ) 
 ) 
14. Bg5 ( { PH: The computer really wants to play } 14. c5 $1 Bc7 15. Ra1 { and White takes control over the a-file with a large advantage. } ) 
Be7 $2 { Trading dark-squared bishops will only worsen Black’s problems. } ( { Better was } 14... Nf6 15. cxd5 ( { but not } 14... Be7 14. c5 $6 Ne4 $1 16. Qc1 Nxg5 17. Nxg5 Be7 { when Black is just fine } ) 
cxd5 16. Ne5 Bd7 17. Ra1 $14 { and White has a pleasant advantage. } ) 
15. Bxe7 Qxe7 16. cxd5 { A timely opening of the c-file. } ( { Another strong option was } 16. c5 { turning the c6-pawn into a permanent target, and after } Nf6 17. Ra1 Bd7 18. Ne5 $16 { White dominates, and is likely to win the a-file with } Ra3 { or Ra5 . } ) 
cxd5 17. Qc7 $6 { Conceptually correct, but tactically imprecise. } ( { Better was } 17. Qc6 $1 { preventing ...Nb6-c4 blocking the c-file: } Rb8 18. Rfc1 Rb6 ( { but not } 18. Qc7 Nb6 $2 19. Qxb5 $18 ) 
19. Qc7 Rb7 20. Qa5 Qd6 21. e3 $16 { followed by Bf1 attacking the b5-pawn. } ) 
Ra4 $6 { Returning the favour. } ( { Better was } 17... Qd8 $1 { preparing ...Nb6-c4 . Then after } 18. Rfc1 Nb6 19. Ne5 Nc4 { the c-file is blocked, though after } 20. Qc5 $5 { ⩲ White retains a small advantage. } ) 
18. Ne5 $1 { ± White has obtained a dream position in the Stonewall; the dark-squared bishops are off the board, the knight has taken control of e5, and the c-file is open for a rook invasion. } Qd8 { Heading for a near-lost endgame. } ( { It does not help to keep queens on the board with } 18... Qe8 19. Rfc1 Nxe5 20. Qxe5 Ra7 { since after } 21. Rc5 $5 { ± followed by Rbc1 and Rc7 , only White has attacking chances. } ) 
 ( { Also } 18... Rxb4 $4 { loses to } 19. Nc6 { . } ) 
19. Rfc1 Qxc7 20. Rxc7 Nxe5 ( { It is too late for } 20... Nb6 21. e3 Nc4 { due to: } 22. Nxc4 dxc4 23. Bc6 $1 { ± } ) 
21. dxe5 { Black is rid of his weakness on e5, but the e6- and b5-pawns remain vulnerable. } Rd8 22. e3 Bd7 23. Bf1 { Threatening Rxd7. } Raa8 ( 23... Kf8 $2 { can be met by } 24. Rxd7 $1 Rxd7 25. Bxb5 Raa7 26. Bxd7 Rxd7 27. b5 { with a winning endgame, though some work is required. } ) 
24. Rbc1 Kf8 25. Rb7 $6 { Natural, but imprecise. } ( { The most accurate was } 25. R1c5 $1 Rab8 26. Ra7 { threatening to double rooks on the seventh rank, forcing } Ra8 27. Rxa8 Rxa8 28. Bxb5 $16 { with excellent winning chances. } ) 
Rab8 26. Rcc7 Rxb7 27. Rxb7 Bc6 $6 { Inaccurate; now White is in full control again. } ( { A much better defence was } 27... g5 $1 { since after } 28. Bxb5 Bxb5 29. Rxb5 d4 $1 30. exd4 Rxd4 { Black has drawing chances due to the weak e5-pawn. } ) 
28. Rb6 Rc8 29. Bxb5 Bxb5 30. Rxb5 $18 { This endgame is already lost, but still instructive. } Rc1+ 31. Kg2 Rb1 32. Rb8+ Kf7 33. Rb7+ Kf8 ( { Black’s king is awkwardly placed after } 33... Kg6 34. h4 { . } ) 
34. h4 Rb2 35. b5 Rb1 36. b6 h6 ( { Or } 36... Rb4 37. h5 Rb3 38. Kf1 Rb2 39. Ke1 Kg8 40. Kd1 { and preventing White’s king penetration will lead Black into zugzwang. } ) 
37. Rb8+ Kf7 38. h5 $1 { Fixing the kingside pawns. } Ke7 ( 38... g6 $2 { loses to } 39. Rb7 Kg8 40. hxg6 { . } ) 
39. b7 Kf7 40. f4 $1 ( { Black resigns as he is helpless against the continuation: } 40. f4 Rb5 41. Kf3 Rb2 42. g4 fxg4 43. Kxg4 Rb3 44. f5 exf5 45. Kxf5 Rb5 46. e6 Ke7 47. Rg8 Rxb7 48. Rxg7 { Final remarks 1. Black’s problems started early in the game; his chances could be improved with 9... Nd7. Instead 9...a5 was not enough to prevent the queenside expansion, and paradoxically, the opening of the a-file helped White in most variations. 2. White’s queenside attack with b2-b4-b5 proved to be a sound plan, provoking Black to play ...b7-b5. This advance prevented the b4-b5 break, but turned the b5-pawn into a serious weakness. 3. Black’s biggest mistake was trading dark-squared bishops, emphasizing the uselessness of his light-squared bishop, and giving up control of the e5-square. } ) 

 1-0

