 { Ognjen Cvitan – Darko Doric, Rijeka 2006 Learning objective: This game illustrates how White may slowly constrain Black’s forces to eventually win through a kingside invasion. } 1. d4 d5 2. c4 e6 3. Nf3 c6 4. e3 Bd6 5. Bd3 f5 6. O-O Nf6 7. b3 Qe7 8. Bb2 O-O ( { PH: An antidote against the 9.Qc1 plan is to delay castling with } 8... b6 9. Qc1 Bb7 10. Ba3 Bxa3 11. Qxa3 Qxa3 12. Nxa3 Ke7 { , keeping the king in the center. It's still slightly more pleasant for White, but Black's position is pretty decent. } ) 
9. Nc3 { The beginning of an interesting plan. } ( { Another popular option is to insist on trading dark-squared bishops with } 9. Qc1 b6 10. Ba3 { and now Black may allow the exchange with } Nbd7 ( { Or prevent the exchange with } 10. Nc3 c5 $5 11. dxc5 ( { PH: Here } 11. cxd5 $1 exd5 12. Nc3 { is pretty bad for Black, a poor structure and Nb5 is coming. } ) 
bxc5 12. Nc3 Ba6 { with level chances as in Petrik – S. Zhigalko, Aix-les-Bains 2011 } { _ } ) 
11. Bxd6 Qxd6 12. cxd5 Nxd5 13. Nbd2 Bb7 14. Nc4 Qe7 15. Nfe5 Rac8 16. Nxd7 Qxd7 17. Ne5 Qe7 18. Qb2 c5 { when Black had solved all his problems in Delchev – Tregubov, France 2009. } ) 
Ne4 10. Ne2 { This move prepares Ne5 followed by f2-f3 which will give White good control of the centre. } Nd7 11. Ne5 Nxe5 12. dxe5 Bc7 ( { Another alternative was: } 12... Bc5 13. Bd4 { Preparing f2-f3. } ( { Also an option is the untested } 12... Bc7 12. Nd4 $5 { ⩲. } ) 
Ba3 { With the idea of ...c6-c5 . } 14. Rb1 ( 13... Bc7 13. f3 { is met by } c5 $1 15. cxd5 ( 14... Bc7 14. fxe4 $6 dxe4 $15 ) 
exd5 16. fxe4 cxd4 17. exd5 Qxe5 18. exd4 Qe3 19. Kh1 Bd6 { with a comfortable position for Black. } ) 
b6 15. Bb2 Bxb2 16. Rxb2 Bb7 17. f3 Nc5 { With level chances in Olszewski – S. Zhigalko, Puerto Madryn 2009. Note that Black’s light-squared bishop will come into the game after ...c6-c5 . } ) 
 { This is the first critical moment in the game. Black’s main problem is to find a good use for the light-squared bishop. The typical ...Bd7-e8-h5 manoeuvre will not benefit Black since White simply responds with f2-f3. Hence, the only alternative is to place the bishop on the a8-h1 diagonal and advance ...c6-c5. On the other hand, White’s objective will be to play c4-c5 obtaining a lasting advantage by restraining Black’s bishop permanently. } 13. Rc1 { A logical move, supporting c4-c5. } ( { Another logical alternative was } 13. Qc2 $5 { also supporting c4-c5, and now after } Bb6 ( { better was } 13. Rc1 Bd7 14. Rac1 Rac8 15. c5 b6 { fighting against the c5-pawn } ) 
14. Rac1 Bd7 15. c5 $1 { White gained a lasting advantage: } Bc7 ( 15. Rc1 Bxc5 $4 16. Bxe4 $18 ) 
 ( { or } 15. Rc1 Nxc5 $4 16. Ba3 $18 ) 
16. f3 Ng5 17. Bd4 Rfb8 18. a4 b6 19. b4 Qd8 20. Rb1 bxc5 21. Qxc5 a5 22. b5 $16 { Shariyazdanov – Beshukov, Biel 2002. } ) 
Bd7 14. f3 Bb6 $6 { This move turns out to be a crucial waste of two tempos. } ( { If } 14... Ng5 $6 { then } 15. c5 $14 { restricts the light-squared bishop, and is similar to the game. } ) 
 ( { A good alternative was } 14... Nc5 15. Bb1 ( { or } 14... Bb6 14. Ba3 Bxe5 16. cxd5 Bd6 { where Black is just fine; } ) 
 ( { more precise is } 14... Bb6 14. cxd5 $5 cxd5 16. Ba3 Bb6 17. Nd4 { with a tiny advantage for White } ) 
dxc4 16. Rxc4 Na6 $1 { when Black equalizes after ...c6-c5 , bringing the light-squared bishop into the game. } ) 
15. Nd4 Ng5 ( { If } 15... Nc5 16. Ba3 $14 { and the pin is somewhat annoying. } ) 
16. Qc2 { Threatening c4-c5. } a5 $6 { This is a rather inaccurate move, though it made no difference in the game. The reason I do not like this move will become clear in my note to White’s 19th. } ( { If } 16... c5 { (preventing c4-c5) then } 17. Nxf5 $5 ( { or the simple } 16... a5 16. Ne2 Rac8 18. Nf4 $14 { when Black’s centre is somewhat vulnerable } ) 
exf5 18. cxd5 c4 19. Bxc4 Bxe3 20. Kh1 { when the position is rather unclear, but White’s chances are certainly preferable. } ) 
17. c5 Bc7 18. Ne2 $14 { White has a lasting positional edge thanks to his spatial advantage and Black’s restricted light-squared bishop. } b5 $6 19. a3 $6 { This is positionally correct, but White misses a stronger continuation. } ( { The reason I did not like 16...a5 is because after } 19. cxb6 $1 { it is not possible to recapture ...axb6; thus after } Bxb6 20. Bd4 { White gains control of the key c5-square, say after: } Rab8 21. Bc5 Bxc5 22. Qxc5 Qxc5 23. Rxc5 Nf7 24. f4 $16 ) 
Nf7 $2 { This is a colossal mistake that should be well understood by the reader. Before playing this move Black had reasonable fighting chances, since White’s pieces could not be mobilized so easily. The e5-pawn was vulnerable, hence natural moves, like moving the bishop away from b2 or playing Nd4, were impossible. If White had played f3-f4, then Black could have responded with ...Ne4. } ( { It was better to prepare ...b5-b4 with } 19... Rfb8 { , for example: }  ( 19... Nf7 19. b4 Ra7 { when Black can create some counterplay down the a-file, or } ) 
 ( 19... Nf7 19. Bd4 b4 21. axb4 ( 20... Nf7 20. Ra1 $5 { ⩲ was better. } ) 
Bc8 { followed by ...Ba6 with a reasonable position for Black. } ) 
 ) 
 { But now that Black has played ...Nf7, the following move is logical and very strong: } 20. f4 $16 { Black’s knight does not have a route back to e4, hence weakening the e4-square is not a problem at all. From this point I think Black’s position is nearly lost, and the reader should pay close attention to how White manages to gradually increase his advantage without giving Black any hope of counterplay. } Nh6 21. Nd4 Rab8 22. h3 { Covering the g4-square and preparing an expansion with g2-g4. } g6 ( { Maybe it was better to attempt something like } 22... b4 23. a4 $6 ( { of course it is better to play } 22... g6 22. Ra1 $5 { ± } ) 
Qe8 24. Rf2 Bc8 25. g3 Ra8 26. Rg2 Ba6 { trading the light-squared bishop. } ) 
23. Rf2 Kf7 24. Nf3 Ke8 25. g3 { Cvitan seems to be enjoying himself. He takes his time, but every move brings him one step closer to the win. Now he is preparing Rg2 and g3-g4. } Kd8 26. Rg2 Kc8 27. g4 Bd8 ( { Of course } 27... fxg4 28. hxg4 { only worsens Black’s pawn structure. } ) 
28. Kh2 { Again, White does not hurry to open the position yet, as there is no need to do so. Instead, Cvitan slowly arranges his pieces in optimal locations. } Bc7 29. Qd2 Be8 30. Kg1 Bd7 31. Qe1 Be8 32. b4 { This is played in order to support the c5-pawn, as White wants to use his rook on the kingside. } a4 { Having a closed queenside, the rest is just a matter of technique. } ( { Opening the a-file with } 32... axb4 $6 33. axb4 { does not exactly help Black, for example: } Ra8 ( 33... Kb7 34. Bd4 Ra8 35. Bxb5 $1 { Opening lines for attack. } cxb5 36. c6 $1 Bxc6 37. Rgc2 $18 ) 
34. Ra1 Rxa1 35. Qxa1 Kb7 36. Bc3 { Followed by Ra2 with a winning attack. } ) 
 ( { It was probably better to keep the tension with } 32... Ra8 $5 { but after } 33. Ra1 $5 { threatening a3-a4, Black does not have anything better than } a4 { , which is similar to the game. } ) 
33. Bc3 $1 { +- This move is logical and very strong. It might seem premature, but after this move Black’s position is already clearly lost, for the following reasons: 1. White has a significant spatial advantage, allowing him to manoeuvre freely with his pieces. 2. Only White can open the position favourably, which he will do only once all his forces are ready for the final invasion. 3. Black’s light-squared bishop is locked in and cannot contribute to Black’s play. 4. With the last move, White shows the beginning of the winning strategy. His first step is to play Bc3-e1-h4 to make good use of his bad bishop. } Kb7 34. Qe2 Bd8 35. Be1 Ka6 36. Bh4 Qc7 37. Bxd8 Qxd8 { The first stage is complete. White has exchanged his bad bishop for Black’s good bishop; note how weak the dark squares in Black’s camp have become. } 38. Nd4 Qe7 39. gxf5 ( { A strong alternative was the direct } 39. Qd1 $1 { threatening Qxa4 , and now } Ka7 40. Bxb5 $1 cxb5 41. c6 { gives White a winning attack, since Black’s forces lack space and coordination to defend: } Ka8 42. Rc5 { (threatening Nxb5) } Rb6 43. g5 Nf7 44. Qc2 { (threatening c6-c7) } Kb8 45. Rxb5 Rxb5 46. Nxb5 Bxc6 47. Qxc6 ) 
Nxf5 40. Bxf5 gxf5 ( { If } 40... exf5 { White can progress with: } 41. Qf2 Kb7 42. h4 Bd7 43. Qg3 Rbe8 44. Qg5 Qf7 ( { or } 44... Qxg5 45. Rxg5 ) 
45. Kf2 { Followed by Rh1 and h4-h5 winning. } ) 
 { The second stage is complete; White has opened the ^g-file and removed all strategic impurities from the position by trading his bishop for Black’s knight. We are left with a winning battle between White’s strong knight and Black’s useless light-squared bishop. It is impossible to prevent the invasion down the ^g-file. } 41. Kh2 Kb7 42. Rcg1 Bg6 43. Rg5 { Progressing slowly but surely. } ( { A more direct winning approach was: } 43. Rxg6 $5 hxg6 44. Rxg6 Rg8 45. Rxe6 Qg7 46. Qf2 Rh8 { (threatening ...Rxh3 followed by ...Rh8) } 47. Qg2 Qxg2 48. Kxg2 Rbg8 49. Kh2 ) 
Rg8 44. Qf2 Rbe8 45. Qh4 Kc7 ( { If } 45... Qg7 { preventing } 46. Qh6 { , then } 46. R5g3 Kc7 47. Qg5 { followed by h3-h4-h5 winning. } ) 
46. Qh6 { Threatening the decisive h3-h4-h5. } Qf8 47. Qxf8 Rgxf8 48. h4 Re7 49. Kh3 { At this point pretty much every plan will win. White continues to take his time and improve his position slowly. } ( { Another option was } 49. h5 Be8 50. Rg7 Kd7 51. h6 { since } Bg6 { fails due to } 52. Rxe7 Kxe7 53. Nxc6 Kd7 54. Nd4 { . } ) 
Kd7 50. h5 Be8 51. Kh4 h6 52. Rg8 Ref7 ( { If } 52... Rxg8 53. Rxg8 Rh7 { White has the nice } 54. Rg6 $1 Bxg6 ( { or } 54... Bf7 55. Rf6 { zugzwang ! } Bg8 56. Rf8 Bf7 57. Ra8 $18 ) 
55. hxg6 Rh8 56. Kh5 Ke7 57. g7 Rg8 58. Kg6 $1 h5 59. Nf3 { followed by Kh7 and wins. } ) 
53. Rxf8 Rxf8 54. Rg7+ Rf7 55. Rg6 Re7 ( { No better is } 55... Rh7 56. Rxe6 { . } ) 
56. Rxh6 Rg7 57. Rxe6 Rg4+ 58. Kh3 Rg1 59. h6 { Final remarks 1. Black’s first mistake was allowing White to play c4-c5, which prevented ...c6-c5, hence restricting the light-squared bishop. This was essentially an opening imprecision. It was necessary to play 12...Bc5 or 14...Nc5. 2. The second mistake was to play 19... Nf7 allowing 20. f4 reinforcing White’s central dominance. This mistake was essentially decisive. 3. White’s win was slow but very convincing and a great model of how to exploit the advantage. Improving Black’s play after move 20 would provide more resistance, but probably would not change the overall result. } 
 1-0

