 { Emilio Cordova – Igor Naumkin, Livigno 2010 Learning objective: This game illustrates Black’s chances for a direct checkmating attack. } 1. d4 Nf6 2. Nf3 e6 3. e3 b6 4. Bd3 Bb7 5. O-O Be7 6. c4 O-O 7. b3 d5 8. Bb2 Nbd7 9. Qe2 a6 10. Rd1 Ne4 11. Ne1 $6 { Not exactly a mistake, but certainly heading in the wrong direction. White intends to play f2-f3 in order to chase away Black’s strong knight on e4. } ( { A healthier alternative was } 11. Ne5 f5 12. f3 Nxe5 13. dxe5 Nc5 { with roughly equal chances in the game Cordova – Ordaz Valdes, Monterrey 2010. } ) 
f5 $1 { A good decision, reinforcing the knight on e4, and going into a Stonewall-type position under favourable circumstances. The reader should note two essential characteristics in this position: 1. Black’s knight is already on e4, creating some chances for a kingside attack. 2. Black’s light-squared bishop can always become active after a timely ...dxc4 opening the a8-h1 diagonal. For these two reasons, White should be particularly cautious, and not expect the usual solid position against the Stonewall. } 12. f3 $6 { A careless move based on a tactical oversight. } ( { It was better to play } 12. Nd2 { though after } Bd6 { Black has a comfortable position. } ) 
Bd6 { My favourite move in the game. This position perfectly illustrates Black’s attacking potential. White wanted to push the knight away in order to obtain a comfortable positional advantage, but Black simply leaves it there. Is it possible to take it? } 13. fxe4 $2 { White does not want to admit that 12.f3 was simply a weakening move, hence he accepts the challenge. Unfortunately for him, Black’s attack is even stronger than it seems. } ( { It was better to accept the mistake and play } 13. Nc3 { after which Black gains the initiative with: } Qh4 14. f4 ( { but not } 14. g3 $2 { because of } Nxg3 $1 15. hxg3 Bxg3 { when White is completely helpless against the threat of ...Rf6-g6 , and } 16. Ba3 { hoping to respond to 16... Rf6 with 17. Be7, is simply answered by } c5 ) 
Ndf6 { The position is about equal, but it is Black who can fight for the advantage. } ) 
fxe4 14. Bc2 Qg5 { A very strong move, preparing ...Nf6-g4 to which White does not have a satisfactory defence despite having an extra piece. Black’s pawns on d5 and e4 are the key as they restrict the mobility of White’s forces. } ( { It is possible that White expected } 14... Qh4 $2 15. g3 ( { but not } 14... Qg5 14. h3 $4 Qg3 { followed by ...Qh2# } ) 
Qh3 { threatening to double rooks on the ^f-file and now } 16. Qg2 Qh6 17. Qe2 Qh3 { leads to a draw by repetition. } ) 
15. Nd2 ( { At first sight, White seems to defend with: } 15. g3 { But after: } Nf6 { Black threatens ...Ng4 and ...Rf2 or ...Nh5xg3 . Now White’s problems become more apparent. A possible continuation is: } 16. Ng2 Nh5 ( { But not } 16. Nd2 Ng4 $2 17. Nf4 $1 { . } ) 
17. Rf1 ( 17. g4 Rf3 $1 { and Black wins after either } 18. gxh5 ( { or } 18. Nd2 Qxg4 { . } ) 
Qxh5 ) 
Nxg3 $1 18. hxg3 Qxg3 { Threatening ...Qh2# . } 19. Rf4 g5 { Winning the rook. } ) 
 ( { The best defence was trading bishops with: } 15. Ba3 Bxa3 ( { or } 15. Nd2 c5 16. Nd2 Nf6 { with a strong attack } ) 
16. Nxa3 Nf6 17. cxd5 ( 17. h3 $2 Nh5 $19 { threatening ...Ng3 and ...Rf1 } ) 
exd5 18. Nd3 { White has to return the piece sooner or later. } exd3 19. Bxd3 Ng4 $17 ) 
Nf6 16. Nef3 { The only move; White really does not have any other way to bring his pieces back to the defence, and a knight on f3 is very much needed to protect h2, and to block the f-file. } ( { Even the similar move } 16. Ndf3 $2 { loses to: } Qh5 { (threatening ...Bxh2+) } 17. h3 ( { or } 17. Rd2 exf3 18. Nxf3 dxc4 19. bxc4 Ng4 20. h3 Rxf3 $1 21. gxf3 Nh2 $19 ) 
exf3 18. Nxf3 Ne4 { threatening ...dxc4 and ...Ng5 (or ...Ng3) (.) } ) 
exf3 17. Nxf3 Qh5 { The attack continues; the threat is ...Bxh2+. } 18. Re1 ( { Worse is } 18. h3 Ne4 $19 { as in the note above. } ) 
dxc4 { Even Black’s light-squared bishop, the famous 'bad bishop” in the Stonewall, comes into the attack. } 19. e4 { White has nothing better than giving up a pawn in order to block the bishop on b7. } ( { White cannot recapture with } 19. bxc4 { because of } Ng4 { with a crushing attack. } 20. h3 Rxf3 $1 21. gxf3 ( 21. hxg4 $4 Qh2 ) 
Nh2 22. Rf1 Nxf3 { Mate is coming soon. } ) 
cxb3 20. axb3 { Black’s strategy has been a success: he has an extra pawn for no compensation, and all his pieces actively contribute to the attack. I will leave the rest of the game with shorter comments as it is less relevant to the subject of this chapter. } Bb4 21. Rf1 a5 22. Rac1 c5 { Undermining White’s control of the centre. } 23. Bd3 $6 ( { A better defence was: } 23. Rcd1 cxd4 24. Bxd4 Bc5 $17 ) 
Rad8 { The d4-pawn has become vulnerable. } 24. h3 $2 ( { Weakening the position decisively. It was safer to 'sit and wait” with } 24. Rc2 { although Black can make progress slowly with: } Rd7 25. Rd1 Rfd8 $17 ) 
Qh6 $1 { Making room for the knight transfer ...Nh5-g3 (or f4) . } 25. Bb1 Nh5 $19 { Black’s multiple threats decide the game. } 26. Ne5 Nf4 27. Qg4 Bd2 28. Kh2 Nh5 ( { But not } 28... Bxc1 $4 29. Bxc1 $16 { losing the knight on f4 . } ) 
29. g3 ( 29. Rcd1 { is useless because of: } Rf4 $1 30. Qe2 ( { or } 30. Rxf4 Qxf4 31. Qxf4 Bxf4 32. Kg1 Bxe5 ) 
Rxf1 31. Rxf1 Bf4 32. Kg1 Ng3 ) 
Ba6 { Winning the ^f-file. } ( { But not } 29... Bxc1 $4 30. Bxc1 { when Black’s queen is trapped. } ) 
30. Rxf8+ ( 30. Bd3 Bxd3 31. Nxd3 Bxc1 32. Bxc1 Qg6 { and wins. } ) 
Rxf8 31. Rg1 Be3 32. Rg2 Bf1 { The rest is simple. } 33. Nd7 Rd8 34. dxc5 bxc5 35. Bf6 Rxd7 36. e5 Nxf6 37. Qxe6+ Rf7 38. exf6 Qxf6 { Final remarks 1. The key to Black’s successful attack was his e4-pawn, which prevented White from bringing pieces into the defence. 2. It is worth studying how Black’s attack was conducted while having control of the centre. Imagine how difficult it would have been to create threats if White had been able to place a knight on e5. } 
 0-1

