g6 { With approximately level chances. } ) 
cxd5 12. Rc1 Nc6 { The reader should note that White cannot really play for more than equality in this position. The knight on e5 is not particularly useful, and Black’s bishop on e8 is not more restricted than White’s bishop on b2. } 13. Nb5 Bb4 14. a3 Ba5 15. Be2 a6 16. Nc3 Ne4 17. b4 ( { But not } 17. f3 $2 { losing a pawn after: } Nxc3 18. Bxc3 Bxc3 19. Rxc3 Nxe5 20. dxe5 Qxa3 $17 ) 
Bc7 18. Nxe4 $6 { It is generally inconvenient for White to take on e4 before placing a pawn on f4. } ( { It was healthier to accept equality with } 18. f4 Nxe5 19. fxe5 Bc6 20. Nxe4 fxe4 { with a probable draw. } ) 
fxe4 { Now Black has pressure down the ^f-file, with the f2-pawn being quite vulnerable. } 19. Qb3 ( { Again } 19. f4 { was superior: } exf3 20. Nxf3 Bh5 { The light-squared bishop comes into the game ! } 21. Qd2 Rf7 { With a microscopic advantage for Black. } ) 
 ( { If } 19. Nxc6 $6 Bxc6 { Black has excellent attacking chances on the kingside, for example } 20. Qd2 Rf6 21. f4 ( { or } 21. g3 Qf7 $17 { followed by ...Rf8 } ) 
exf3 22. Rxf3 Rxf3 23. Bxf3 Qh4 24. g3 Qg5 $15 { with the threat of ...h7-h5-h4 . } ) 
Bxe5 20. dxe5 { The e5-pawn is now a permanent weakness. } Qg5 21. Kh1 $6 { It was necessary to prevent Black’s next move. } ( { Better was: } 21. Qd1 $1 { Preventing } Bh5 { , and in a way saving the e5-pawn tactically. } Rf7 { Preparing ...Nxe5 . } ( { If } 21. Kh1 Nxe5 $6 { White gains compensation for the pawn after: } 22. Rc7 Rf7 23. Rxf7 Nxf7 24. f4 $5 ) 
 ( { But } 21. Kh1 Rd8 $5 { allows Black to keep a tiny advantage. } ) 
22. Bg4 Bd7 23. Bh5 $1 { This curious move is the key. } g6 ( { Or } 23. Kh1 Re7 24. Qg4 $1 { . } ) 
24. Be2 { White is holding, since } Nxe5 $2 { now fails to: } 25. h4 $1 Qf6 26. Qd4 $18 ) 
Bh5 $1 { ∓ I find it difficult to believe that throughout the past four moves Aronian seems to have been heading in this direction voluntarily. He had a clear chance to equalize with 18.f4, and even 19.f4 was better than this position. Black’s strategy has succeeded in every way. Let’s spell out why: 1. Black does not have to worry about a weak e5-square. Instead, White has a weak e5-pawn to worry about. 2. There is pressure down the ^f-file, thanks to the strong pawn on e4. 3. Black has managed to activate his bad bishop with ...Bd7-e8-h5. 4. White’s bishop on b2 is practically useless at the moment. } 22. f3 ( { Trading light-squared bishops seems quite dangerous: } 22. Bxh5 Qxh5 { For example: } 23. Qd1 ( { Instead, the careless } 23. h3 $2 { is met by the strong: } Rf3 $1 24. Kh2 ( 24. gxf3 $4 Qxh3 25. Kg1 exf3 $19 ) 
Raf8 { (threatening ...Rxh3 followed by ...Rf3 with forced mate) } 25. Qd1 Rxh3 26. gxh3 Rf3 27. Qxf3 Qxf3 $19 ) 
Qh4 { Keeping queens on the board. } ( { Also interesting was } 23. f3 Qg5 $5 { . } ) 
24. Rc2 Rf5 { Preparing ...Rh5 . } 25. f4 exf3 26. gxf3 ( 26. Rxf3 $2 Raf8 $17 ) 
Qh3 $5 { ⩱ } ) 
 ( { Even worse is } 22. Qd1 $2 Bxe2 23. Qxe2 Rf7 $17 { since the e5-pawn is about to fall. } ) 
Qh6 { A prophylactic move, preparing ...exf3 followed by doubling rooks on the ^f-file. } ( { The variation } 22... exf3 23. gxf3 Rf5 $2 24. f4 $1 { illustrates the importance of playing } Qh6 { . Now 24...Qh6 } 25. Qc2 { and White solves most of his problems. } ) 
23. Rce1 exf3 24. gxf3 Rf7 25. Bc1 ( { No better is } 25. f4 Bxe2 26. Rxe2 Ne7 $1 27. Rg2 Nf5 $17 { since the bishop on b2 is no opponent for a knight on f5 . } ) 
 ( { An alternative was } 25. Qd3 { but after } Ne7 { (threatening ...Nf5) } 26. e4 Qf4 $17 { White’s position is very uncomfortable. } ) 
Bg6 $6 { There is nothing wrong with this move, but there was a more immediate win. } ( { Black could have decided the game with the precise: } 25... Nxe5 $1 26. e4 Bxf3 $1 { This shot was probably missed by both players } ( { instead } 26... Qf6 $2 { gives away most of the advantage after } 27. exd5 exd5 28. Bb2 $15 ) 
27. Bxf3 Qh3 { Black goes into a winning endgame after the relatively forced line: } 28. exd5 Rxf3 29. Rxf3 Nxf3 30. Bf4 Qg4 31. Rf1 Qxf4 32. Rxf3 Qc1 33. Kg2 Qg5 34. Rg3 Qd2 35. Kh3 exd5 $19 ) 
26. e4 Qh3 27. exd5 Nd4 28. Qd1 Nxe2 29. Qxe2 Bh5 $1 { The f3-pawn is now lost. } ( { But not: } 29... exd5 $2 30. e6 Rc7 31. Rf2 $15 ) 
30. Kg1 Bxf3 31. Qf2 Qg4+ { An interesting alternative; instead of deciding the game immediately through tactical means, Tomashevsky heads into a won endgame, where he displays his positional mastery in an impeccable way. } ( { A more tactical approach was } 31... Raf8 $1 32. dxe6 Qxe6 33. Qg3 Bc6 { with a winning attack. } ) 
32. Qg3 Qxg3+ 33. hxg3 Bxd5 $19 { The endgame is won, since White has absolutely nothing to aim for, while Black has many pawns to attack, such as a3, e5 and g3. Throughout the remaining part of the game, the reader should notice how Black’s ‘bad’ bishop is actually extremely strong, while White’s bishop is relatively useless in most variations. The position is not exactly a model of the Stonewall anymore, but it certainly deserves study as an example of endgame technique. } 34. Be3 a5 35. b5 a4 { Disconnecting the queenside pawns. Tomashevsky knows his pawns on b7 and a4 cannot be captured, hence he will always have pawns on both flanks, which is often a prerequisite for winning. } 36. Rxf7 Kxf7 37. Rf1+ Kg6 38. Rf4 h6 { Black can take time to improve his position before taking decisive measures. } 39. Kf2 Bb3 40. Rg4+ Kh7 41. Rd4 Rc8 42. b6 Rc2+ 43. Ke1 ( { More stubborn was } 43. Rd2 { but after } Rc3 44. Bd4 Rc8 $5 { I could not find a way to hold White’s position, for example: } 45. Ke3 Bd5 46. Rb2 Rc1 47. Kd2 ( { or } 47. Rd2 $2 Rg1 $19 ) 
Ra1 48. Bc5 g5 $5 49. Rc2 Rh1 50. Rc1 Rh2 51. Kd3 Kg6 { Followed by ...Kh5-g4 . } ) 
Ra2 { And now White’s position simply falls apart. } 44. Bc1 ( { Instead } 44. Rd7 $2 { loses on the spot after: } Rxa3 45. Rxb7 Bd5 $1 { Attacking rook and bishop. } 46. Bc1 Rxg3 $19 ) 
Rg2 45. Bf4 Rg1+ 46. Kd2 Ra1 { The a3-pawn falls, and the rest is simple for Black. } 47. Kc3 ( { Not } 47. Rd7 $4 Rd1 $19 { . } ) 
Rxa3 48. Kb4 Ra1 49. Bd2 ( 49. Rd7 Bd5 $19 ) 
Bd5 50. Bc3 Ra2 51. Rd3 Kg6 52. Rd4 a3 53. Rd3 Kf5 54. Bd2 Ra1 55. Bc3 Ra2 56. Bd2 Ke4 57. Re3+ Kd4 58. Bc1 Rc2 { Final remarks 1. The usefulness of Black’s bishop manoeuvre is best illustrated in the note to White’s 19th move. Aronian was probably dissatisfied with this position and so chose a different alternative which proved to be worse. 2. In general, the pawn structure transformations which occurred on moves 18-20 do not help White, as the e5-square becomes unavailable and particularly the e5-pawn becomes a target while Black lacks clear weaknesses. } 
