 { Dronavalli Harika – Michael Adams, Gibraltar 2010 Learning objective: This game is an example of how Black may create central pressure with ...b7-b6 and ...c6-c5. } 1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 e6 3. g3 d5 4. Nf3 Bb4+ 5. Bd2 Be7 6. Bg2 O-O 7. O-O c6 8. Qc2 b6 9. Rd1 Nbd7 10. Bf4 ( { PH: Not long before this book was published, I had an interesting game against Adams in the same line at the World Cup. The game went } 10. b3 Ba6 11. a4 Ne4 12. Be1 f5 { , achieving the Stonewall structure. After } 13. a5 Bd6 { I played the lazy } 14. e3 { , being a little afraid of Black playing ...f4. However, this was not a threat in this position. } ( 14. Nc3 $1 Qe7 15. Ra2 $1 { ⩲ would keep some advantage, preparing to double on the a-file. } ) 
Qe7 ( { Adams could have countered my plan of playing on the a-file with } 14. Bf4 b5 $1 { , when } 15. c5 Bc7 16. Nc3 g5 $15 { Black is generating some initiative on the kingside. } ) 
15. Nc3 ( { Now } 15. Bf1 $1 { would have brought unpleasant tension along the f1-a6 diagonal for Black. } ) 
Ndf6 16. Ra2 Nxc3 17. Qxc3 c5 18. axb6 axb6 19. Rda1 Bb7 { and here I offered a draw with } 20. Rxa8 { which was accepted. } ) 
Bb7 11. Nc3 Nh5 12. Bc1 { Planning to redevelop the bishop with b2-b3 followed by Bb2. } f5 { We have reached the position of interest via a well-known theoretical line. Something to note about this position is that, unlike previous examples, Black’s pieces are very well arranged for a ...c6-c5 break. Black’s bishop is already on b7, the rook is coming to the ^c-file, and White is far from controlling the e5-square. Hence, Black has a clear way to find counterplay and make use of his ‘bad’ bishop. } 13. b3 ( { Black has a perfectly solid position after } 13. cxd5 cxd5 14. Bd2 Rc8 15. Rac1 b5 16. Qb3 a6 17. a4 b4 18. Na2 a5 { with a level game. PH: After } a6 { the computer says that White gets an advantage with } 17. e3 { , intending to maneuver the c3-knight to f4: } Nhf6 18. Ne2 { and perhaps now threatening to exchange dark-squared bishops with Bb4. So } Rc4 { and now White insists on this idea with } 19. a3 Ne4 20. Bb4 $14 ) 
Rc8 14. e3 Bd6 { Black rearranges his pieces for the break ...c6-c5, while always keeping an eye on the e5-square. } ( { The break } 14... c5 $2 { is premature due to the simple } 15. cxd5 exd5 16. Bb2 { threatening dxc5 or Ne5 . Black’s centre is far too vulnerable: } g6 ( 16... c4 $2 { loses to } 17. Ne5 $1 ) 
17. Qd2 { (preparing dxc5) } cxd4 ( { again } 17... c4 18. Ne5 $1 { ± } ) 
18. Nxd4 Nc5 19. Nde2 $16 { Winning a healthy pawn after 20.Nxd5 . } ) 
15. Bb2 Nhf6 { Notice how White cannot easily carry out her typical plans. There is no way to expand the queenside or trade dark-squared bishops. Hence, all that is left is keeping an eye on the e5-square with: } 16. Ne2 Qe7 17. Nf4 ( { If } 17. Ne5 Bxe5 18. dxe5 Ng4 { there is no defence for the e5-pawn, though after } 19. a4 $5 Ngxe5 20. Ba3 Nc5 21. a5 { White should have enough compensation to equalize. PH: I thought at first that after } 19. a4 $5 { Black would be able to block the queenside with } a5 20. Ba3 c5 { , but then after } 21. Nf4 { the pawn on d5 remains hanging - White is also in very good shape here. } ) 
Rfd8 18. Rac1 Ne4 19. Nd3 $2 ( { PH: The correct move is } 19. Ne5 $1 { - compare with the variation given at White's 17th move. Black's best reply seems to be } c5 20. Nxd7 Rxd7 21. cxd5 Bxf4 { (otherwise Black is left with a weak isolated pawn on d5) } 22. dxe6 Qxe6 23. d5 $1 Bxd5 24. exf4 $14 { and the strong dark-squared bishop spells danger for Black's king. } ) 
Bb8 { There is no hurry to break in the centre, as White does not have a clear-cut plan to make use of the weak e5-square. } ( { Another option was } 19... c5 $5 20. dxc5 Ndxc5 21. Nde5 Nd7 { with level chances. } ) 
20. Nfe5 ( { If } 20. c5 bxc5 ( { the waiting move } 20. Nfe5 Qf7 $5 { is interesting too } ) 
21. Nxc5 Nexc5 22. dxc5 Rf8 23. b4 e5 { yields a complex position with mutual chances. } ) 
Nxe5 21. Nxe5 ( { PH: I would not have guessed, but this is a considerable strategic mistake. Better was } 21. dxe5 $1 dxc4 22. bxc4 c5 { when Black threatens to infiltrate the light squares around White's king with ...Ng5, so } 23. h4 $1 { , preventing the threat, after which the knight on e4 is a bit stuck. } ) 
c5 $1 { Black’s pieces are ideally placed for this break, which undermines White’s control of the e5-square. } 22. Qe2 ( { Chances are equal after } 22. cxd5 Bxd5 23. Qe2 cxd4 24. Bxd4 { . } ) 
cxd4 ( { PH: Even stronger was } 22... Bxe5 $1 23. dxe5 dxc4 $1 24. bxc4 Ng5 $1 { ∓ - compare with the variation 21.dxe5. } ) 
23. exd4 $6 { Allowing Black to create weaknesses in White’s pawn structure. } ( { Better was } 23. Bxd4 dxc4 24. Nxc4 { with level chances. } ) 
Bxe5 24. dxe5 dxc4 25. bxc4 ( { Or } 25. Rxc4 Rxd1 26. Qxd1 Rxc4 27. bxc4 h6 $1 { followed by ...Qc5 with a small edge. } ) 
Qb4 $1 { White’s strategy has proved to be a complete failure, and she must begin to fight for equality since: 1. The e5-square is no longer weak. 2. Black’s light-squared bishop is no longer bad. 3. White’s c4-pawn is a permanent weakness. } 26. Bd4 Rd7 ( { More precise was } 26... h6 $1 { making a bolthole for Black’s king, while keeping the central pressure: } 27. Qc2 Rd7 { White is in trouble since there is no ideal defence against ...Rdc7 or ...Rcd8 . For example: } 28. Qb3 $2 ( { or } 27... Rd7 27. Be3 Rxd1 29. Qxd1 Rxc4 ) 
Qxb3 29. axb3 Rcd8 $19 ) 
27. Be3 Rcd8 28. Rxd7 Rxd7 29. Qe1 ( { Black has only a microscopic advantage after } 29. Qc2 h6 30. Qb3 Qe7 { since White’s pawns are somewhat vulnerable. } ) 
